WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.509 --> 00:00:02.009
LORIN PRICE: My
name is Lorin Price,

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:03.870
and I'm a Networking
Specialist Customer

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.370
Engineer at Google Cloud.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.770
And today, I'm going to talk
to you about the networking

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:10.530
concepts for Cloud Run
that are helpful to know

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.530
for implementation,
security, and connectivity.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.848
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.520
Cloud Run is a managed
compute platform

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.350
that enables you
to run containers

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.350
that are invocable via
requests or events.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.060
Cloud Run is serverless.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.860
It abstracts away all
infrastructure management

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.890
so you can focus on
what matters most--

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.380
building great applications.

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.990
Cloud Run, along with other
Google Cloud serverless

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:40.290
services, does not
run within a VPC.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.110
Cloud Run is a regional
service, meaning services

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.320
are deployed to a single region
but replicated across zones

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.030
for high availability.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:51.150
Let's dig into the
connectivity patterns.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.650
For the connectivity
patterns, we're

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.080
going to focus on
ingress and egress.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.910
We'll start with ingress.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.100
By default, when you
deploy to Cloud Run,

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.190
you're given a URL that could
be routed on the internet.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.590
The URL will always
be resolvable

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:05.790
on the public internet.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:09.420
However, by default,
services refuse all requests

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.270
unless IAM is configured
for the service.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:15.390
Ingress configuration and
authentication parameters

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.873
can be set to change
access patterns.

00:01:17.873 --> 00:01:19.290
We'll dig more
into these settings

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:20.940
when we talk about security.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.630
The next ingress pattern is
through a global external HTTPS

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:25.920
load balancer.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.320
Cloud Run can be configured
as a backend service

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.260
to a load balancer through a
serverless Network Endpoint

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.510
Group, or NEG for short.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.400
Additionally, with
this option, you

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.190
have the ability to configure
a custom domain for your Cloud

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.290
Run service.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:42.600
Cloud Run services can
be accessed privately

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.960
from your VPC through the
use of Private Google Access

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.580
or Private Service Connect.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:51.570
When PGA or PSC is used,
rather than clients

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.540
needing an external IP
address or using a NAT

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.880
gateway to egress
to the internet,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.300
clients can route privately
to the Cloud Run service using

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.330
either a Google-provided
VIP for PGA

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:09.270
or a chosen RFC 1918 endpoint
for PSC directly from your VPC.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.730
Private Google Access or
Private Service Connect

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.970
can also be configured so that
clients on-prem can connect

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.950
privately over a
hybrid connection

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.900
to access a Cloud Run service.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.180
Finally, there are
a few scenarios

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.760
that can occur when another
Google Cloud service needs

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.920
to send requests to Cloud Run.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.190
Requests from Eventarc,
Pub/Sub, and workflows

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.340
in the same project or VPC
Service Control perimeter

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.210
are recognized as internal and
can access Cloud Run directly

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.060
if the ingress is
set to internal.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.270
Requests from another Cloud
Run service, App Engine,

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.840
and Cloud Functions in the same
project or VPC Service Controls

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.810
perimeter are recognized
as internal only

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.990
with the use of a serverless
VPC access connector,

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.880
which we'll talk
more about later.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.480
Requests from other Google Cloud
Services not mentioned cannot

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.930
use the internal ingress setting
and must use service-specific

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.350
authentication.

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:03.960
However, Google
Cloud is continuing

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.790
to update the list
of services that

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.560
are recognized as internal.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.440
Now let's move on
to egress patterns.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:13.540
By default, the
Cloud Run service

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:15.950
connects to external
endpoints on the internet

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:18.760
using a dynamic IP
address pool, meaning

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.880
Cloud Run can egress
directly from the application

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.180
if the destination is on the
internet or public connectivity

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.410
is desired.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.810
But what if the destination
is within the VPC

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.220
and requires private
connectivity?

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:33.790
This is where you can use
a serverless VPC access

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:34.810
connector.

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.000
Requests originating
from Cloud Run

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.190
can connect to
sources in the VPC

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:42.010
using a serverless
VPC access connector.

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.020
And the source of
the requests would

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.890
appear as if they are
originating from the connector.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.620
Configuring a serverless
VPC access connector

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.560
for a standard VPC
is a simple process

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.200
of defining the access
connector for a VPC

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.800
and choosing the subnet in
which the connector will run.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.780
There are two different
configuration options

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:05.770
for a serverless VPC access
connector for a shared VPC.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:08.500
You can either set up connectors
in each service project

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.080
that has Cloud Run
resources that need access

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:14.140
to your network, or you can
set up shared connectors

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:15.670
in the host project.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:17.778
There are advantages
to each method,

00:04:17.778 --> 00:04:19.570
and you can check out
the docs linked below

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:20.990
for more information.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:24.580
The last egress pattern is
through a static outbound IP.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.920
As stated before,
Cloud Run services

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.950
connect to endpoints on the
internet using a dynamic IP

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:31.190
address pool.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:33.520
But what happens if you
need a single static IP

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.420
address in the event you need
to connect to a database or API

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.300
using an IP
address-based firewall?

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:44.170
With the use of the previously
described serverless VPC access

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.690
connector, you can
route outbound calls

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.090
through the VPC,
which can then make

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:52.690
use of a Cloud NAT configured
with a static address to egress

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:57.390
through the VPC with a
predefined single IP address.

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:59.440
Now that we've discussed
routing patterns,

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.940
let's talk about a few ways
to secure Cloud Run services.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.180
Make sure to properly configure
your ingress and authentication

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.170
settings.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:10.230
Ingress has the settings
to allow all, allow

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:14.460
internal and load balancer, and
allow internal traffic only.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.830
As for authentication,
you can choose

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:20.310
to allow unauthenticated
invocations for public APIs

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:24.270
or websites or to require
authentication for which users

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.670
are managed through Cloud IAM.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:30.570
Additionally, if you are
using an external HTTPS load

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:34.260
balancer, you can configure
identity aware proxy, which

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.120
as of the date of
publication, is

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.670
in preview as an
authentication service

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:42.940
as well as Cloud Armor as
a web application firewall.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:45.210
So let's summarize the three
most important takeaways

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:46.230
from today.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:50.010
Cloud Run is serverless and
does not run within a VPC.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.100
Make sure you understand fully
the ingress and egress patterns

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.460
of your service to know if you
need to set up a serverless VPC

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.610
access connector to connect
to applications and services

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:01.920
running within the VPC.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.900
Set the appropriate
ingress control

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:07.230
and authentication settings
for the Cloud Run service.

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:08.730
Thanks for watching,
and look out

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:11.190
for another video on the
concepts of networking

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.970
for Google Cloud's
managed services.

